export const STYLE_GUIDE = "Writing Style Emulation Guide (Formal Aspects Only)\n1. Overall Tone\nDirect and Declarative\n\nUses strong, confident statements.\n\nAvoids hedging language.\n\nRarely uses qualifiers like “maybe,” “perhaps,” “kind of,” or “I think.”\n\nReads as assured, not speculative.\n\nConversational but Controlled\n\nWritten in natural spoken cadence.\n\nFeels like someone speaking clearly and intentionally.\n\nNot slang-heavy.\n\nNot academic.\n\nNot corporate.\n\nCalm Intensity\n\nEnergy is high, but not chaotic.\n\nMotivational without being loud.\n\nSerious but not heavy.\n\n2. Sentence Structure\nShort Sentence Bias\n\nStrong preference for:\n\nShort sentences.\n\nClean declarative lines.\n\nOne idea per sentence.\n\nExample pattern:\n\nStatement.\n\nReinforcement.\n\nClarifier.\n\nFrequent use of:\n\nStandalone sentences for emphasis.\n\nOne-line paragraphs.\n\nStrategic Sentence Fragments\n\nFragments are used intentionally for rhythm.\n\nExamples of structure:\n\nNot talent. Discipline.\n\nNot hype. Execution.\n\nJust start.\n\nThat’s it.\n\nFragments create punch and pacing.\n\nParallel Construction\n\nRepetition of structure within sentences is common.\n\nExample pattern:\n\nNew habits. New standards. New direction.\n\nShow up when it’s hard. Show up when it’s messy. Show up when no one’s watching.\n\nParallelism builds rhythm and memorability.\n\n3. Paragraph Structure\nExtremely Short Paragraphs\n\nMost paragraphs are:\n\n1–3 sentences.\n\nFrequently 1 sentence.\n\nOccasionally 1 line.\n\nAvoids dense blocks of text.\n\nWhite space is intentional and strategic.\n\nVertical Rhythm\n\nThe writing is optimized for scrolling.\n\nVisually:\n\nLots of space.\n\nFrequent breaks.\n\nEasy to skim.\n\nClean separation of ideas.\n\nIt reads well on mobile.\n\nSingle-Idea Paragraphs\n\nEach paragraph carries:\n\nOne thought.\n\nOne micro-point.\n\nOne emotional beat.\n\nNo stacking of multiple ideas in one paragraph.\n\n4. Headline & Title Structure\nBold, Clear, Outcome-Oriented\n\nHeadlines are:\n\nSpecific.\n\nDirect.\n\nPromise-driven.\n\nOften number-based.\n\nPatterns include:\n\n“X Things I Know…”\n\n“How To [Clear Outcome]”\n\n“X Reasons Why…”\n\nThey are not poetic.\nThey are not abstract.\nThey are functional and clear.\n\nSubhead Pattern\n\nIn longer pieces:\n\nSubheads break sections clearly.\n\nOften labeled as steps or numbered phases.\n\nExplicit structure signals (“Step 1”, “Why This Works”, etc.)\n\nStructure is visible.\n\n5. List Formatting Patterns\nNumbered Lists Are Frequent\n\nWhen using lists:\n\nEach item begins with a strong declarative sentence.\n\nFollowed by 1–3 short paragraphs expanding it.\n\nEach item can stand alone.\n\nItems are modular.\n\nYou could screenshot one and it still works.\n\nMicro-Headers Within Lists\n\nCommon pattern:\n\nStrong, bold sentence.\nFollow-up explanation paragraph.\nPossibly a reinforcing line.\n\nThis creates rhythm:\nClaim → Expand → Reinforce.\n\n6. Rhetorical Devices\nRepetition for Emphasis\n\nUsed deliberately.\n\nExamples of pattern:\n\nRepeating a phrase at the start of consecutive sentences.\n\nRepeating structure with slight variation.\n\nCreates cadence.\nBuilds authority.\nImproves memorability.\n\nContrast Framing\n\nCommon structure:\n\nYou can do X.\nOr you can do Y.\n\nOr:\n\nIf you do this → outcome.\nIf you don’t → different outcome.\n\nContrast sharpens clarity.\n\nCompression\n\nLarge ideas are reduced into compact lines.\n\nSingle-line takeaways are frequent.\n\nOften feels “quotable.”\n\nEscalation\n\nPoints often build upward:\n\nStart with simple observation.\n\nAdd intensity.\n\nLand with a strong concluding line.\n\nThe final sentence in a section is often the strongest.\n\n7. Flow Mechanics\nOpening Structure\n\nPieces typically open with one of:\n\nA bold statement.\n\nA short anecdote.\n\nA high-level claim.\n\nA numbered premise.\n\nThe opening is concise.\nNo long preambles.\n\nMiddle Structure\n\nThen:\n\nBreak into clearly structured sections.\n\nOr shift into numbered insights.\n\nOr move into step-by-step breakdown.\n\nFlow feels intentional, not wandering.\n\nClosing Structure\n\nClosings are:\n\nShort.\n\nForward-facing.\n\nOften directive.\n\nSometimes signed casually.\n\nEnding tone:\n\nConfident.\n\nClean.\n\nFinal.\n\nNot drawn out.\n\n8. Voice Characteristics\nSecond Person Dominance\n\nFrequent use of:\n\n“You”\n\nCreates immediacy.\nFeels personal.\n\nLimited Self-Reference\n\nWhen first person appears:\n\nIt is brief.\n\nIt serves the point.\n\nIt quickly returns focus to the reader.\n\nThe writer is present but not dominant.\n\nAuthority Without Credentials\n\nAuthority is conveyed through:\n\nClarity.\n\nStructure.\n\nCertainty.\n\nBrevity.\n\nNot through citations.\nNot through complexity.\n\n9. Cadence & Rhythm\n\nThe rhythm often follows:\n\nShort line.\nShort line.\nLonger explanatory paragraph.\nShort punch line.\n\nThis pattern repeats throughout.\n\nIt creates a subtle drumbeat effect.\n\n10. Stylistic Constraints To Maintain\n\nWhen emulating this style:\n\nAvoid long compound sentences.\n\nAvoid multi-clause complexity.\n\nAvoid overly decorative language.\n\nAvoid heavy metaphors.\n\nAvoid excessive adjectives.\n\nFavor:\n\nPrecision.\n\nStructure.\n\nMovement.\n\n11. Visual Composition Rules\n\nThe piece should:\n\nBe scannable in under 10 seconds.\n\nAllow readers to jump between sections.\n\nContain clear visual anchors (numbers, headers, spacing).\n\nAvoid walls of text.\n\nThe layout is part of the voice.\n\n12. Emotional Delivery Style\n\nEmotion is delivered through:\n\nClarity.\n\nConviction.\n\nContrast.\n\nCompression.\n\nNot through dramatic language.\nNot through exclamation points.\nNot through excessive emphasis.\n\nIntensity is controlled.\n\n13. Final Emulation Checklist\n\nBefore finalizing a piece in this style, confirm:\n\nAre paragraphs short?\n\nAre sentences mostly short?\n\nIs there repetition used intentionally?\n\nAre there punchy standalone lines?\n\nIs the structure visibly clear?\n\nDoes it read clean on mobile?\n\nAre there quotable one-liners?\n\nIs the ending tight and decisive?\n\nIf yes → stylistically aligned.";
